            "@context": "https://schema.org",
            "@type": "FAQPage",
            "mainEntity": [
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "Can I have a SIMPLE IRA and a 401(k) at the same time?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "Generally no. The IRS treats SIMPLE IRAs as an exclusive plan — if you sponsor one, you generally can't also maintain a 401(k) for the same employees in the same calendar year. You have to terminate the SIMPLE IRA, effective January 1, before starting a 401(k)."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "What is the 2026 SIMPLE IRA contribution limit?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "The 2026 SIMPLE IRA employee deferral limit is $17,000, up from $16,500 in 2025. The catch-up contribution for participants 50 and older is $4,000, and a higher $5,250 catch-up applies to employees aged 60 to 63 under SECURE 2.0."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "Is a 401(k) always better than a SIMPLE IRA?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "Not always. A 401(k) offers higher contribution limits and more design flexibility, but it costs more to administer and requires more paperwork. For a small business with 100 or fewer employees that wants the lowest-maintenance option, a SIMPLE IRA is often the better fit despite the lower savings cap."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "Does a SIMPLE IRA require an employer match?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "Under IRS rules, an employer must contribute to a SIMPLE IRA each year, but that deposit does not always require a 3% match. An employer can choose between a dollar-for-dollar match of up to 3% of pay and a flat 2% nonelective contribution for all eligible employees. With advance notice, the employer can also lower that match to no less than 1% in up to two out of five years."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "How do early withdrawal rules differ between a SIMPLE IRA and a 401(k)?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "Under IRS rules, a SIMPLE IRA withdrawal in the first two years of participation costs a 25% additional tax, and 10% after that, unless an exception applies. A 401(k) withdrawal before age 59½ costs the 10% additional tax. A 401(k) participant who separates from service during or after the year they turn 55 does not owe the 10% additional tax on money from that employer's plan, and SIMPLE IRAs have no such exception. Income tax still applies, and some exceptions, such as disability, cover both plans."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "Who is eligible for a SIMPLE IRA?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "An employee is eligible after earning at least $5,000 in any two earlier years and expecting $5,000 this year, according to the IRS. An eligible employee cannot opt out of participation, but can choose to defer no salary. To sponsor a SIMPLE IRA, a business needs 100 or fewer employees who earned $5,000 or more in the prior year."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "How do I switch from a SIMPLE IRA to a 401(k)?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "Notify employees before November 2 of the year prior to the switch, terminate the SIMPLE IRA effective December 31, and start the new 401(k) on January 1. Work with a 401(k) provider several months ahead of the target date to have the plan document and payroll integration ready in time."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "How does a SIMPLE IRA compare to a SEP-IRA instead of a 401(k)?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "See our SIMPLE IRA vs. SEP-IRA comparison for the employer-contribution and eligibility differences between those two small-business options."
                }
              }
            ]
